@*
    PROTOTYPE — throwaway. Variant B, "Split deck".

    No poster and no dark ground: the page keeps the app's own light palette, the words take the
    left half, and the books are a fanned deck on the right — overlapping, tilted, the way books
    actually sit when someone puts a pile down. Nothing slides; the deck breathes.

    The bet: the app is a quiet, papery thing, and a landing screen that looks like the app is
    more honest than one that looks like an advertisement for it.
*@

<section class="p-split">
    <div class="p-split__words">
        <p class="p-eyebrow p-eyebrow--ink">@Greeting</p>
        <h1 class="p-display p-display--ink">ReadingTracker</h1>
        <p class="p-lede p-lede--ink">
            Track what you are reading, and how far through it you are.
        </p>
        <div class="p-actions">@CallToAction</div>
    </div>

    <div class="p-deck" aria-hidden="true">
        @* Back to front, so the one on top is the last in the source. *@
        @foreach (var (book, position) in Deck)
        {
            <div class="p-deck__card" style="--at: @position">
                <PrototypeCover Book="book" Real="RealCovers" />
            </div>
        }
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string Greeting { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public bool RealCovers { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? CallToAction { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A handful, not the whole list: a deck is a pile you could pick up.</summary>
    private static IEnumerable<(PrototypeBook Book, int Position)> Deck =>
        PrototypeBooks.All.Take(6).Select((book, position) => (book, position));
}
